s and betting markets aligned on a comfortable Republican win.", "watch": "If Republican softness on Blackburn deepens or David Hatley's self-funded independent bid draws conservative votes, the margin could tighten.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Open seat: Bill Lee (R) is not on the ballot."]}, "overview": "Tennessee opens its governor's race with Republicans in a strong position. The state leans Republican by about fourteen points, Bill Lee is term-limited, and Senator Marsha Blackburn enters the general election with far greater name recognition than state Representative Jerri Green. Polls since late summer show Blackburn ahead by nine to twenty-one points, and betting markets give Green roughly a five percent chance. Nearly every nonpartisan race rating calls the contest solidly Republican.\n\nThere are modest signs of friction. A plurality of voters tell pollsters they would prefer someone else, some Republicans are lukewarm on Blackburn, and independent David Hatley has put five million dollars of his own money into the race. Green has also campaigned actively across the state. Still, none of these factors has moved polls or ratings materially, and the expected outcome is a double-digit Republican win.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.0257, "margin": -11.12}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.0463, "margin": -10.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.0186, "margin": -12.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.0193, "margin": -12.0}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.0323, "margin": -11.0}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.0336, "margin": -11.0}, {"date": "2026-09-28", "p_dem": 0.0245, "margin": -12.0}, {"date": "2026-09-29", "p_dem": 0.009, "margin": -14.0}, {"date": "2026-09-30", "p_dem": 0.0321, "margin": -11.0}, {"date": "2026-10-01", "p_dem": 0.0183, "margin": -13.0}, {"date": "2026-10-02", "p_dem": 0.0272, "margin": -12.0}, {"date": "2026-10-03", "p_dem": 0.0281, "margin": -12.0}, {"date": "2026-10-04", "p_dem": 0.0357, "margin": -11.5